        "result": "2026-Q1 nonfarm unit labor costs increased 2.3%, with hourly compensation +3.1% and productivity +0.8%. 2026-Q2 unit labor costs increased 1.3%, with compensation +2.7% and productivity +1.4%. Later revisions appearing elsewhere are excluded from the forecasting sample."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view base rate is the mean of six distinct preliminary prints from 2025-Q1 through 2026-Q2: 11.8 / 6 = 1.9667%. Their range is -1.9% to 5.7%. The two latest preliminary prints average (2.3 + 1.3) / 2 = 1.8%, supporting a modest downward update."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level effect: use the historical mean as the central tendency of quarterly growth; no absolute index-level adjustment is applied. Momentum effect: blend toward the recent two-print mean, consistent with moderating preliminary compensation growth and stronger productivity. One-off effect: retain the extreme historical prints in dispersion, but apply no separately quantified future shock. Policy mechanism: policy can affect compensation and output, but no separate policy adjustment is estimated."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Supplemental attempts to extend the sample into 2024 returned access-denied pages, and that retrieval branch was stopped. Infrastructure and internal errors on other lookups supplied no evidence. No unavailable observations were invented; the forecast uses the six successfully fetched preliminary prints."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a mean-reverting persistence model with analyst-selected equal weights on the six-print historical mean and the latest two-print mean. Prior = 1.9667; momentum adjustment = 0.5*(1.8 - 1.9667) = -0.0833; level, one-off and separate policy adjustments = 0. Point = 1.8833, rounded to 1.9%. Because this is a growth series, calculate dispersion from the print values themselves: sigma = 2.4606 percentage points = sqrt(30.2733333/(6-1)). The normal-approximation 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 3.1496. Thus 1.9 +/- 3.1496 gives [-1.2496, 5.0496], rounded to final bounds [-1.25, 5.05]. No additional widening or narrowing is applied."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk is a compensation rebound combined with stalled or falling productivity; a sufficiently large combination would land above 5.05%, outside the interval. Downside risk is another strong productivity surge with subdued compensation, as illustrated by the negative 2025-Q3 preliminary print; a larger negative outcome would land below -1.25%, outside the interval. The six-observation sample limits interval calibration."
      },
